**Read in full:** [Certification of gas-mixture reference materials — NIST SP 260-222](../sources/Gas-Mixture-Reference-Materials-NIST.lingua#r_471GDGAT44EMNYFBERQSMVE2C8) and [Hybrid humidity generator — NIST SP 250-83 Revision 1](../sources/Hybrid-Humidity-Generator-NIST.lingua#r_6C1ZNNWH3JHFPV8CNY0FW26BGQ), with the quantitative analyzer sections of [Total and Partial Pressure Measurement — Karl Jousten](../sources/Total-and-Partial-Pressure-Jousten.lingua#r_7SH9NGBWVVK5YN24S2VZPTW45M).

Choose a trace impurity in a defined gas matrix and follow reference preparation/value assignment, delivery, sampling, response and inference. Specify the stream interval represented by the result. A sensitivity or detection claim must include the matrix, blank, response time and interference evidence that make it meaningful.

Compare two genuinely different measurement responses. Use dilution, recovery, blank sequences and changed concentration to separate calibration error from sampling loss and surface memory. Where a spectral matrix is used, investigate rank, conditioning and uncertainty in the calibration itself.

Propagate common reference and flow errors through the calibration points. Show how a plausible model can fit the same observations yet imply a different impurity amount. Design a further observation that distinguishes the models before making an acceptance claim.

**Demonstrate:** A traceable composition account, justified uncertainty, explicit detection/decision statements and reproducible calculations. Preserve unresolved evidence and explain how the claim changes after a source, regulator, sampling line or operating condition is changed.

**Return:** [Industrial gases, vacuum and high-purity supplies — from molecules to useful service](../Industrial-gases-and-vacuum.lingua#r_3GHQJEYMYEN6JDKSF4PPJ403RF).
**Continue in gas and vacuum systems:** [Review what the measurements establish about the service](Review-what-the-measurements-establish-about-the-service.lingua#r_00STQ667X1Y8PNYK73F7YC4AXE).

